{"kind":"request","text":"Bound resident memory growth with sequence depth (corrected replacement for 17516cf4).","body":{"conditions":"Replaces request 17516cf4, which asserted a 31 KB per record slope and a PREVIEW miss that the measurements did not support. Corrected evidence at main 529758ca (docs/reference/performance.md): resident RSS grows with depth because the linear-history projection retains every application event, but the slope is not pinned down. The one clean same-scenario pair, harness depths 100 and 257 at 29 MB and 39 MB, gives 63.7 KB per record over a short baseline; comparing depth 100 to depth 2,000 across scenarios gives 36.8 KB. PREVIEW therefore lands between 1.8 and 3.2 GB against a 1 GiB target and FIRST-PRODUCTION between 18 and 32 GB against 4 GiB, so both miss at every point in the range even though the slope is unsettled. Memory figures are the load-invariant ones on that page: two harness runs at load 3 and load 20 returned timings differing threefold and peak-memory figures identical to within one percent. Suspected bounding component: full retention of application events in the projection. Satisfied when the per-record slope is established by measurement across at least a decade of depth, and a resident serving 50,000 records stays under 1 GiB peak RSS.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}